{"reply":"No—it doesn’t prove either claim. It supports a narrower, more useful conclusion: fear of consequences may have influenced your silence, but it may not have been the only factor, and your choice cannot establish what “anyone” would have done.

The gardeners’ concealment is a strong analogy because their fear of an explicit, extreme punishment plainly shapes what they do. But your situation may also have involved uncertainty about reporting expectations, embarrassment, a sense that fixing the mistake was enough, or assumptions about how your team would respond. The error-reporting research makes the team-environment explanation plausible: it links willingness to report with psychological safety, safety climate, and leader inclusiveness—but it studied broader patterns, not your particular motive or team. [Study](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)

